+# Decision: Reconciling Concurrent Edits to One Visit
+
+## Context
+
+Two field workers can correct the same plot-visit independently, days apart, after comparing notes while their tablets were offline for up to 48 hours. The record store must define what a single current visit means when more than one complete edited copy arrives, without treating arrival order as evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative.
+
+A visit bundles an observation date, an observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some of these fields are scientifically related — a species identification and its cover estimate belong together — and the server cannot infer that relatedness from field names. A regional science partner receives a reviewed CSV each Friday; two coordinators, with no dedicated systems administrator, resolve questions about submitted observations. Roughly 6,000 visits arrive per season.
+
+The reconciliation mechanism sets the submission envelope, the shape of coordinator review work, and which visit states may enter the export, so tablet-sync and review-export are sized around it. Two failure modes define the stakes: losing a submitted edit silently, and combining related field changes into a record that looks resolved but is scientifically wrong.
+
+Both candidate designs retain original submissions — the tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* is a standing invariant here, not a point of difference — and both support offline capture without real-time collaboration.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+The criteria below drive selection. Both options clear the standing invariants above, so no eliminating gate applies; judgment rests on the relative weight of these criteria.
+
+- **Scientific-correctness risk under related-field edits — major** (tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*). Can the mechanism emit a record that welds scientifically related values — a species identification from one worker with a cover estimate from another — without a human reviewing that join?
+- **Sustained maintenance burden — major** (tenet: *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*). Does the mechanism require authored, maintained per-field merge rules and field-level provenance that must stay correct as the visit schema evolves?
+- **Review-queue volume and export readiness — moderate**. How many visits need coordinator attention before the Friday export, and how many are export-ready with no intervention? A larger queue delays the export and consumes the two coordinators.
+- **Tablet payload and local state — minor**. How much data does each submission carry, and what must a tablet retain between edits: a base revision ID, or a full base snapshot plus structured changes?
+- **Cost of adopting merging later — minor**. If the team later wants automatic field combination, how much of the record model, tablet sync, and export must be rewritten? A whole-revision model is built into all three.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each edit submits a complete visit as a new revision naming the revision it started from (its base revision). A visit is a chain of such revisions with a single head. When two or more edits name the same base — whether they touch the same field or unrelated fields — they become competing complete revisions. Nothing is merged and nothing is promoted automatically; the visit is unresolved until a coordinator selects a winning revision. Non-selected revisions are retained in history and are never export-eligible. A resolved visit's head is the single current value, and only a resolved visit may enter an export.
+
+**Demonstration.** Workers A and B both fetch visit V at revision r2. A corrects an observer name; B adds a missing species. Both submit complete visits based on r2. The store holds two competing revisions, r3a and r3b, and marks V unresolved. A coordinator views both, selects one as the head, and V becomes resolved and export-eligible; the other remains in V's history.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific-correctness risk under related-field edits: **strong**. No field is ever taken from one submission and joined to another, so a species identification and its cover estimate always come from the same submitted visit. Every exported value is one intact worker submission.
+- Sustained maintenance burden: **strong**. There are no merge rules to author or maintain; correctness does not depend on classifying fields as scalar, list, or species entry.
+- Review-queue volume and export readiness: **weak**. Even edits to unrelated fields create competing revisions and therefore a review item, so more visits reach the coordinators and fewer are export-ready without intervention.
+- Tablet payload and local state: **partial**. A tablet retains a base revision ID and submits a whole visit — larger than a delta, but with no base snapshot or structured-change machinery to keep.
+- Cost of adopting merging later: **weak**. The revision-chain model, the tablet's base-revision tracking, and the export's whole-visit comparison are all built around complete visits; adding field combination later rewrites all three.
+
+**Summary.** Preserves whole-visit intent and needs almost no merge logic, at the price of a larger review queue.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each edit supplies the base state it started from and a structured set of field changes, not a whole visit. The server combines changes to disjoint fields automatically and routes overlapping changes to a coordinator for review. Merge rules define how each field type behaves: scalar fields such as observation date, observer lists, and species entries each need a rule for combining concurrent edits. Field-level provenance records which submission supplied each resulting value. A record is export-ready automatically when all its edits are disjoint, and enters review when fields overlap.
+
+**Demonstration.** With the same visit V at r2, A's observer-name correction and B's added species touch disjoint fields, so the server merges them into one current record with no review. Had both revised the same species' cover estimate, that overlap would produce a review item showing the two competing values and their provenance.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific-correctness risk under related-field edits: **partial**. Disjoint-field edits combine without review, and the server cannot tell that an added species and a revised cover estimate are scientifically related; a plausible but wrong record can reach a human only if an overlap happens to be detected. Requiring manual approval of every combined record closes the gap but gives up the option's main advantage of a small queue.
+- Sustained maintenance burden: **weak**. Correctness depends on merge rules that must be authored, tested, and kept current for every field type, and on field-level provenance plumbing — ongoing engineering a two-coordinator team without a systems administrator must sustain.
+- Review-queue volume and export readiness: **strong**. Unrelated edits combine automatically, so far fewer visits need coordinator attention and more are ready for the Friday export.
+- Tablet payload and local state: **partial**. A tablet sends compact structured changes but must retain a full base snapshot to compute them, trading submission size for local state.
+- Cost of adopting merging later: **strong**. Field-level provenance and merge rules are present from the start, so extending automatic combination later needs no model rewrite.
+
+**Summary.** Fewer review items and smaller submissions, paid for with maintained merge rules and a silent-error risk on scientifically related fields.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: whole-visit revisions.**
+
+It wins on both major criteria. On scientific-correctness risk it never welds related values across submissions, so the path from an exported value back to one intact submitted visit stays inspectable (tenet: *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*). On sustained maintenance burden it needs no merge rules or provenance machinery that the two coordinators would have to keep correct (tenet: *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*). Retaining non-selected revisions also honors *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*.
+
+It loses on the moderate criterion: unrelated-field edits still create competing revisions, so the review queue is larger and fewer visits are export-ready without a coordinator. That cost is accepted — a visible queue of whole, intact visits is preferred over automatic combination that could join scientifically related edits unnoticed. The "no systems administrator" tenet is leaned on rather than overridden: a bigger review queue is more coordinator work, not a systems-administration dependency.
+
+**What this obligates downstream.** These are cascade obligations on two separate nodes; the designs for tablet-sync and review-export remain their own work.
+
+- **Tablet-sync** retains the base revision ID a worker edited from, submits a complete visit rather than a delta, and displays a visit's pending competing revisions to the worker so an offline edit's outcome is explainable. Because a worker may have been disconnected for days, a tablet can be many revisions behind and must be able to explain that its submission became a competing revision rather than the head. The submission envelope and retry state machine are sized around whole visits, not around base snapshots and structured changes.
+- **Review-export** presents a side-by-side comparison of competing whole-visit revisions, drives the resolution operation that selects a winning revision, records the coordinator and contributing submissions, and holds unresolved visits out of the partner's CSV. Export eligibility is a single resolved/unresolved signal from the record store; the export must link back to that signal rather than invent its own merge or combination semantics. Field-level provenance is not available under this choice, so any per-field explanation the partner needs is a reason to revisit the decision, not something the export can reconstruct.
+
+**Why the other approach stays in consideration.** The decision should be re-run if its premises change: if a larger review queue proves unmanageable for two coordinators before the Friday export, if the server gains a reliable way to detect scientific relatedness between fields, if the science partner requires field-level provenance in the CSV, or if the team acquires engineering capacity that makes merge-rule maintenance cheap. Those conditions would let field-aware merge's strengths — fewer review items, smaller submissions, and field-level provenance — outweigh its silent-error risk. The comparison above is preserved so the judgment can be re-run against changed circumstances rather than re-argued.
