+## Concurrency Policy: Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each edit submits a complete visit as a new revision naming the revision it started from (its base revision). A visit's history is a chain of revisions from the first submitted version to a single head; each revision points to its base. A brand-new visit begins with a revision that has no predecessor.
+
+When an edit is based on the current head and no other edit is pending against that head, the new revision becomes the head and the visit has an unambiguous current state. When two or more edits name the same base revision — whether they touch the same fields or unrelated fields — they become competing revisions. Competing revisions are not merged and none is promoted automatically; the visit carries unresolved review work until a coordinator resolves it.
+
+Because each revision is a complete visit, every submitted edit stays intact as a unit. Even edits to unrelated fields produce competing revisions and therefore review work. The design accepts a larger review queue in exchange for never combining scientific fields on the server's own judgment.
+
+## Data Model: Revisions and Resolution
+
+A revision records the complete visit payload, the submitting worker's identity, the server receipt time, the base revision ID, and the originating submission ID. The original submission payload is preserved separately from the revision representation so provenance survives any later resolution.
+
+A visit is resolved when it has a single head revision and no competing revisions; otherwise it is unresolved. Resolving review work records a resolution outcome: the identity of the coordinator, the IDs of the contributing submissions, and the resulting head revision. Subsequent edits name that head as their base. Competing revisions that were not selected are retained in the visit's history and remain retrievable; they are not discarded. They are never presented as the current state and are not eligible for export.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved representation. A retry with the same idempotency key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result without creating another stored submission; reuse with a different payload is rejected and leaves the original submission unchanged.
+
+Current-state retrieval distinguishes a usable current state from unresolved review work. For a resolved visit it returns the head revision as the current state. For an unresolved visit it indicates that unresolved review work exists and returns the set of competing revisions — with their authors, receipt times, base revisions, and payloads — and the base they diverged from, rather than a single current value. Submission-history retrieval returns the full revision chain including resolutions and retained competing revisions.
+
+## Review Outcome and Export Eligibility
+
+The review-outcome operation records a coordinator's resolution of competing revisions and leaves the visit resolved with a single head. The record store exposes each visit's resolved/unresolved state as the export-eligibility signal: only a resolved visit's head revision is a current value that may enter an export. Eligibility is defined here; the workflow that presents competing revisions to coordinators and the act of producing the CSV belong to review-export.
+
+## Downstream Contract Obligations
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+The contract above sizes the two dependent nodes; their designs are not defined here.
 
